course: dictionary
lesson: 11754
title: Holding on to the banister
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A banister is the rail along the side of a staircase. You hold it when you go up or down, so that you do not fall. Read this: «Hold the banister when you go up the stairs.» The word hold tells you what the banister is for.
explain: «Ben held the banister with both hands as he walked down the stairs in his socks.» Socks are slippery on wood, so Ben holds on for safety. The clue is in the sentence: socks and stairs make it risky, so holding on is the sensible thing to do.
explain: «The old house had a wooden banister that was smooth from years of hands.» Smooth from years of hands means many people have held it over a long time. Try your meaning in the sentence: the rail along the stairs fits, so banister means that rail.
fact: A banister is the rail along the side of a staircase that you hold on to.
fact: Banisters can also mean the rail together with the posts that hold it up.
fact: Banister can also be spelt bannister.
fact: Safety rule: hold the banister when you use the stairs, especially with socks on.
fact: Do not slide down the banister or climb over it, even though stories often show children doing this in big old houses.
exercise: «Hold the banister when you go up the stairs.» What is a banister? | A window at the top of the house | *The rail along the side of a staircase that you hold on to | A step that creaks | A kind of stair carpet
exercise: «Ben held the banister with both hands as he walked down the stairs in his socks.» Why did Ben hold the banister? | To hide behind it | To wave to his mum | To slide faster | *To keep his balance and stay safe
exercise: Find and copy the word that names the rail on a staircase: «The old house had a wooden banister that was smooth from years of hands.» | *banister | house | smooth | wooden
test: Which is the safest way to use a banister? | Run down the stairs holding nothing | Climb over the banister to get down faster | Slide down it with your socks on | *Hold the banister firmly as you go down the stairs
test: Banisters can mean the rail together with what? | The carpet | The steps | *The posts that hold the rail up | The front door
exam: Why is it dangerous to climb over the banister? | It makes the stairs too bright to see | *You could fall a long way and get badly hurt | It is perfectly safe, so there is no danger at all | It turns the banister into a wall
